				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-5">
					<!-- CHAPTER 1: THE ALGAE'S EYE -->
					<div class="p-5 sm:p-6 rounded-2xl bg-zinc-950/70 border border-zinc-800 hover:border-zinc-700 transition flex flex-col justify-between">
						<div>
							<div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-3">
								<span class="text-xs font-mono text-blue-400 font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest">
									CHAPTER 01 · MOLECULAR GENETICS
								</span>
								<span class="text-lg">🌿</span>
							</div>
							<h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-white mb-2">
								<span class="lang-zh">藻类的眼 · 通道视紫红质-2 的发现</span>
								<span class="lang-en">The Algae's Eye · Discovery of Channelrhodopsin</span>
							</h3>
							<p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-zinc-300/90 leading-relaxed mb-3">
								<span class="lang-zh">
									几十亿年来，单细胞绿藻衣藻（Chlamydomonas reinhardtii）在水体中自由游动，依靠细胞前侧一个小小的橙红色“眼点”寻找阳光进行光合作用。彼得·黑格曼（Peter Hegemann）与格奥尔格·纳格尔（Georg Nagel）在 2002–2003 年破译了这个分子机关：通道视紫红质-2（ChR2）。它将光敏受体与阳离子通道集于一体——只要被约 470 nm 蓝光照射，通道瞬间敞开，钠、钙离子涌入产生电脉冲。自然界在微小藻类中早已备好了操纵电信号的光控钥匙。
								</span>
								<span class="lang-en">
									For billions of years, the single-celled green alga Chlamydomonas reinhardtii has steered through ponds toward sunlight via a microscopic orange-red eyespot. Peter Hegemann and Georg Nagel uncovered its molecular mechanism in 2002–2003: channelrhodopsin-2 (ChR2), an unprecedented protein uniting light sensor and ion channel. Absorption of ~470 nm blue light triggers rapid cation influx, generating an electrical current within milliseconds.
								</span>
							</p>
						</div>
						<div class="pt-3 border-t border-zinc-800/80 text-xs font-mono text-blue-300/90 bg-blue-500/5 -mx-2 -mb-2 p-2 rounded-lg border border-blue-500/20">
							<span class="lang-zh">
								🌊 启示：演化在最不起眼的低等生物体内封存了最精巧的光电引擎，等待好奇的心灵前去解读。
							</span>
							<span class="lang-en">
								🌊 Insight: Evolution hid an all-in-one optoelectronic motor inside pond scum, awaiting discovery.
							</span>
						</div>
					</div>

					<!-- CHAPTER 2: THE 1979 PROPHECY -->
					<div class="p-5 sm:p-6 rounded-2xl bg-zinc-950/70 border border-zinc-800 hover:border-zinc-700 transition flex flex-col justify-between">
						<div>
							<div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-3">
								<span class="text-xs font-mono text-blue-400 font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest">
									CHAPTER 02 · VISIONARY THEORY
								</span>
								<span class="text-lg">📜</span>
							</div>
							<h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-white mb-2">
								<span class="lang-zh">1979 的预言 · 弗朗西斯·克里克的洞察</span>
								<span class="lang-en">The 1979 Prophecy · Francis Crick's Vision</span>
							</h3>
							<p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-zinc-300/90 leading-relaxed mb-3">
								<span class="lang-zh">
									早在 1979 年，DNA 双螺旋共同发现者弗朗西斯·克里克（Francis Crick）在《科学美国人》发表了著名的预言：神经科学面临的终极难题在于无法特异性操纵大脑中成千上万种细胞类型。电极刺激如同“用重锤敲击精密机械”，无法分辨兴奋与抑制；化学药物扩散太慢，动辄需要数秒乃至数分钟。克里克写道：“要想真正理解大脑，我们需要能够随心所欲开关特定类型神经元的方法——而最理想的控制信号，将是光线。”
								</span>
								<span class="lang-en">
									In 1979, Francis Crick (co-discoverer of DNA structure) published a visionary critique in Scientific American: neuroscience was hamstrung by indiscriminate tools. Electrodes act like sledgehammers stimulating everything at once; drugs diffuse over sluggish seconds or minutes. Crick foresaw that unlocking the brain required switching single cell types on and off at will, concluding: "the ideal signal would be light."
								</span>
							</p>
						</div>
						<div class="pt-3 border-t border-zinc-800/80 text-xs font-mono text-amber-300/90 bg-amber-500/5 -mx-2 -mb-2 p-2 rounded-lg border border-amber-500/20">
							<span class="lang-zh">
								💡 预言成真：在克里克写下这段话 26 年后，光终于成为了人类点亮神经元回路的画笔。
							</span>
							<span class="lang-en">
								💡 Prophecy fulfilled: 26 years later, light transformed from a theoretical wish into reality.
							</span>
						</div>
					</div>

					<!-- CHAPTER 3: THE SWITCH IN THE LIVING BRAIN -->
					<div class="p-5 sm:p-6 rounded-2xl bg-zinc-950/70 border border-zinc-800 hover:border-zinc-700 transition flex flex-col justify-between">
						<div>
							<div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-3">
								<span class="text-xs font-mono text-blue-400 font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest">
									CHAPTER 03 · THE BREAKTHROUGH
								</span>
								<span class="text-lg">🐭</span>
							</div>
							<h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-white mb-2">
								<span class="lang-zh">小鼠大脑的开关 · 光遗传学的诞生</span>
								<span class="lang-en">The Brain Switch · Dawn of Optogenetics</span>
							</h3>
							<p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-zinc-300/90 leading-relaxed mb-3">
								<span class="lang-zh">
									2005 年，斯坦福大学的青年神经科学家卡尔·戴瑟罗斯（Karl Deisseroth）与其团队将 ChR2 基因装入无毒病毒载体，成功转导进哺乳动物神经元。当蓝色光纤照向活体小鼠大脑，光开关瞬间生效：一束蓝光闪烁，小鼠神经元以毫秒级时间精度发射动作电位，甚至能直接驱动小鼠按光照节律转弯、奔跑或压制焦虑恐惧。戴瑟罗斯将光学（Optics）与遗传学（Genetics）完美结合，正式确立了“光遗传学”（Optogenetics）这一划时代学科。
								</span>
								<span class="lang-en">
									In 2005, Karl Deisseroth at Stanford University delivered ChR2 into mammalian neurons via viral vectors. Coupling genetics with fiber-optic illumination in freely moving mice, his lab demonstrated millisecond-precision optical control over neuronal firing and behavioral circuits. Deisseroth named and pioneered the field of Optogenetics, handing scientists the remote control of thought and behavior.
								</span>
							</p>
						</div>
						<div class="pt-3 border-t border-zinc-800/80 text-xs font-mono text-blue-300/90 bg-blue-500/5 -mx-2 -mb-2 p-2 rounded-lg border border-blue-500/20">
							<span class="lang-zh">
								⚡ 毫秒精度：告别迟钝的化学弥散，大脑回路的因果逻辑终于可以直接用光来书写与验证。
